1 OCCU The head of state of the Duchy of Courland, Semigallia and Pilten 1812, Landesbevollmächtigter
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Count from 1779 inherited from his father
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karl Johann Friedrich von Medem''' (1762-1827) was a prominent politician of the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia. Remte manor, Vecauce manor (1786), etc. owner of estates. Member of the Courland nobility. Head of the civil gov
2 CONC ernment of the independent Duchy of Courland, Semigallia and Pilten (1812). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Count '''Carl Johann Friedrich von Medem''' (* 1762) was the founder of the count's branch of Rempten and Altautz. He became the Courland state representative and died in 1827 as a lord of Rempten, Cappeln and Wehsaten as well as 
2 CONC a lord of Altautz, Dselen, Behren, Großautz, Kwelen and Weitenfeld. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Curriculum vitae 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on March 23, 1762 in Mežotne manor in the family of Johann Friedrich von Medem and his second wife, Louise Charlotte von Manteifel-Zege, as his fourth child.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Karla Mädem's older half-sister Elizabeth Charlotte Constance became a famous writer, while Anna Charlotte Dorothea's sister married the last Duke of Courland Peter Biron. On December 24, 1788, he married Wilhelmine Browne of Camu
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kārlis Mēdems became the trustee of the Kurzeme chivalry, after his father's death in 1785 he owned Remte, Viesātu, Kapeļu and other manors. In 1800, he started building the current Remte manor palace, in 1804 he also bought the J
2 CONC aunauce manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Courland and Semigallia were again separated from the Russian Empire on August 1 during the war of 1812, Count Karl Johann Friedrich von Medem became the head of the independent duchy's civil government as the trustee of the 
2 CONC Courland chivalry. On December 20, 1812, due to the retreat of Napoleon's army, he lost all his positions and devoted himself to the management of his estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1815, Count Kārlis Mēdems started the construction of the house of the lords of the Jaunauce manor. Died on December 9, 1827 in Jelgava.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1788, Count Carl Johann Friedrich von Medem married Elisabeth Wilhelmini, daughter of Governor-General George Brown, in Riga.[2] Seven children were born to them:
2 CONT
2 CONT Karl von Medem (15.02.1801 - 17.11.1860), heir to the Remte estate;
2 CONT Anna, married von der Ropp (13.11.1789 - 10.10.1807), from 1806 the first wife of Ferdinand von der Ropp, owner of the Biksti estate;
2 CONT Karolina, married von der Ropp (13.07.1794 - 07.02.1872), second wife of Ferdinand von der Ropp, owner of Biksti manor from 1808, heiress of Jaunauce manor;
2 CONT Sophia, married von der Palen (13.11.1789 - 10.10.1807), from 1821 the wife of Johann von der Palen (1784-1856);
2 CONT Dorothea, married von Scheping, wife of Privy Councilor Friedrich Magnus von Scheping (1779-1855);
2 CONT Eliza von Modem. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Genealogisches Handbuch der baltischen Ritterschaften, Teil 3,1: Kurland, Bd.:1, Görlitz, 1939, S.369[obsolete link]
2 CONT 2. Baltic Historical Commission 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karl Johann Friedrich Reichsgraf von Medem''' (March 23, 1762, the Mesothen estate, now Mezotne, Bauska region - December 9, 1827, Mitava) - the ancestor of the count family von Medem from the house of Rempten-Alt-Autz, the fir
2 CONC st owner of the Rempten Majorate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Johann Friedrich von Medem, brother of the diplomat Christoph Johann Medem and the last Duchess of Courland Anna Charlotte Dorothea.
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 1, 1812, by decision of the French Emperor Napoleon I, the independent Duchy of Courland, Semigallia and Pilten was recreated in the territories of the former Duchy of Courland and Semigallia, annexed by the Russian Empi
2 CONC re in 1795, and Karl von Medem headed its civil government. He performed these duties for several months, until on December 20 of the same year, the Napoleonic troops left Courland, which returned to the control of the Russian Emp
2 CONC ire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 1814, the leader of the Courland nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1804 he acquired the estate of Neu-Autz (German: Neu-Autz), in 1815 he began the construction of a manor house. After the death of Medem, the estate passed to his daughter Caroline and her husband Baron Ferdinand von der Ropp f
2 CONC rom the Ropp family[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Awards
2 CONT
2 CONT Order of St. Anne, 2nd class[4]
2 CONT Order of St. Anne, 1st class (August 13, 1817)
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Carl Johann Friedrich von Medem was married to Elisabeth Christina Charlotte Wilhelmine Countess von Broun-Kamus, daughter of Count Georg von Broun-Kamus and his second wife Eleanor Christina, née Baroness Mengden. From this marri
2 CONC age were born:
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Carl von Medem (02/15/1801 - 11/17/1860) [2] - the second owner of the Rempten majorate;
2 CONT Countess Anna von Medem (11/13/1789 - 10/10/1807) [6], married Baroness von der Ropp, from 10/20/1806 - 1st wife of Baron Ferdinand Dietrich Christoph von der Ropp;
2 CONT Countess Caroline von Medem (07/13/1794 - 02/07/1872) [6], married Baroness von der Ropp, from 11/20/1808 - 2nd wife of Baron Ferdinand Dietrich Christoph von der Ropp;
2 CONT Countess Sofia von Medem (04/03/1799 - 03/28/1872) [2], married Countess von der Pahlen, from 08/30/1821 - wife of Count Johan (Ivan Petrovich) von der Pahlen (11/25/1784 - 07/01/1856)
2 CONT Countess Dorothea von Medem, [2] married Baroness von Schepping, wife of Privy Councilor Baron Friedrich Magnus von Schepping (1779-1855)
2 CONT Countess Eliza von Medem
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
