1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ( "Александр родился в 1787 году в семье генерал-поручика князя Сергея Александровича Меншикова (1746—1815) и княжны Екатерины Николаевны Голицыной, одной из первых красавиц своего времени. По утверждению Долгоруко
2 CONC ва, его биологическим отцом был граф Армфельд. У него был младший брат Николай и сёстры Елизавета и Екатерина. Получил домашнее воспитание. Образование он получил в Дрездене.") 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9C%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%88%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2,_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%80_%D0%A1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87 Александр Меншиков] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D1%84%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%82,_%D0%93%D1%83%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%86 Something]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Sergeyevich_Menshikov Prince Alexander Sergeyevich Menshikov (Russian: Алекса́ндр Серге́евич Ме́ншиков; August 26, 1787 – May 2, 1869) was a Finnish-Russian nobleman, military commander and s
2 CONC tatesman. He was made adjutant general in 1817 and admiral in 1833.
2 CONT
2 CONT A great-grandson of Alexander Danilovich Menshikov, Duke of Ingria, and a cognatic descendant of the Princely House of Golitsyn (another of his great-grandfathers was Prince Mikhail Golitsyn, the military governor of Åbo during th
2 CONC e Russian occupation in the Great Northern War). Menshikov entered the Russian service as attaché to the embassy at Vienna in 1809. He became close with Tsar Alexander I and accompanied him throughout his campaigns against Napoleo
2 CONC n. In 1817 Menshikov was appointed acting Quartermaster general of the General Staff. In 1823, he was transferred to the ministry of foreign affairs. Menshikov retired from army service in 1824.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the initiation of the Russo-Persian War of 1826–28 and the prevail of Abbas Mirza's initiative in Tehran, Menshikov was placed under house arrest. He was appointed head of the Naval Headquarters and cabinet minister by Tsar
2 CONC  Nicholas I. He distinguished himself at the Siege of Varna and in 1830 became a member of the State Council. In 1831 Menshikov held the post of Governor-General of Finland. He mainly devoted himself to naval matters. His bad infl
2 CONC uence on the development of the Russian Navy stalled its technical progress and combat training.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1853, Menshikov was sent on a special mission to Constantinople,[1] and when the Crimean War broke out he was appointed commander-in-chief on land and sea. He commanded the Russian army at Alma and Inkerman and showed incompete
2 CONC nce and lack of military talent. On February 15, 1855, Menshikov was removed from command, and replaced by Prince Mikhail Dmitrievich Gorchakov. Between December 1855 and April 1856, he held the post of Governor General of Kronsta
2 CONC dt and then retired. He died in St. Petersburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created Prince (Fürst) in the Finnish nobility, being the only person of the rank of prince to be registered in the Finnish House of Nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Finnish steamship Furst Menschikoff was named after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fi.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleksandr_Sergejevit%C5%A1_Men%C5%A1ikov Aleksandr Sergejevitš Menšikov tai Aleksanteri Menshikov (ven. Александр Сергеевич Меншиков, suom. Aleksanteri Sergeinpoika M., ruots. Alexander Menschikoff , 
2 CONC saks. Alexander Reichsfürst Menschikoff, Suomen ritarihuoneeseen introdusoituna Menschikoff (26. elokuuta 1787 – 2. toukokuuta 1869 Pietari) oli Suomen kenraalikuvernööri, Venäjän meriministeri ja ruhtinas.[
2 CONT
