1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Cronman_nr_748#TAB_9
2 CONT
2 CONT Fritz (son av Hans Detterman, adlad Cronman, tab 1). Student i Dorpat8 1655-05-08. Överstelöjtnant 1668-09-18. Abgesant till Ryssland s. å. 30/9. Överste och generaladjutant i holländsk tjänst. Död före 1680.5 Gift med Christina O
2 CONC ttiliana Börner, levde änka 1712.5
2 CONT Barn:
2 CONT Johan Fredrik, död ogift.
2 CONT Charlotta, levde 1708. Gift med översten och lantrådet Henrik Gotthard von Buddenbrock, till Kaipen, i hans 1:a gifte, född 1648, död 1727 och fader till Henrik Magnus von Buddenbrock, natural. och friherre von Buddenbrock.
2 CONT Lunetta. Gift med guvernören i Maastricht, generallöjtnanten N. N. Villater.5
2 CONT Ottiliana. Gift med lantdrosten i Holland N. N. Dorp.5
2 CONT Sofia. Gift med kaptenlöjtnanten Peter von Gagenholtz, till Laskowitz, som 1695-04-04 blev adlad, men ej introd.3
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/anrep/1/0501.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Överste. Släckt nr. 748 - 1672. Frans Hansson von Cronman (Dettermann). Major of the Swedish Empire, Diplomat. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Överste. Släckt nr. 748 - 1672. Frans Hansson von Cronman (Dettermann). Major of the Swedish Empire, Diplomat. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fritz_Cronman
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Cronman_nr_748#TAB_9
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/anrep/1/0501.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Major Fritz Cronman (circa 1640 - circa 1680) of the Swedish Empire. He was a Swedish diplomat to the Tsardom of Russia from 1668 to 1669. His extant diary and letters contain detailed information on the court of Ivan V of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography: He was born in Swedish Livonia to Hans Detterman Croman. He participated in the border commission with Russia in Keksholm, but the negotiations ended when the Russian commissioners walked out on the talks. On September 
2 CONC 26, 1668 he was appointed as the Swedish diplomat to the Tsardom of Russia under Ivan V of Russia. On October 8, 1668 he sailed to Finland and then traveled overland to Narva. On November 10, 1668 he arrived at the Russian border 
2 CONC at Orlina, where he was escorted to Novgorod. In his letter from Novgorod to the Swedish government he writes: "Russians inevitably must be impelled to such unusual höflighetsbetygelser either under pressure from the enemy side or
2 CONC  from fear of his friends." He arrived in Moscow on December 28, 1668. His Swedish entourage consisted of 35 people. While in Russia, he made a copy of the Godunov map. He married Christina Ottiliana Börner and had a daughter, Cha
2 CONC rlotta Cronman, who married Henrik Gotthard von Buddenbrock (1648-1727). Charlotta and Henrick had as their son, Henrik Magnus Buddenbrock.
2 CONT
2 CONT Research: Researched and written by Richard Arthur Norton (1958- ) for Wikipedia and uploaded to Findagrave on January 8, 2011.
2 CONT
2 CONT . 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fritz_Cronman Fritz Cronman - Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fritz Cronman (c. 1640 - c. 1680) was a Major for the Swedish Empire in the late 17th century, and the Swedish diplomat to the Tsardom of Russia from 1668 to 1669. His extant diary and letters contain detailed information on the c
2 CONC ourt of Ivan V of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Swedish Livonia to Hans Detterman Croman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He participated in the border commission with Russia in Keksholm, but the negotiations ended when the Russian commissioners walked out on the talks. On 26 September 1668 he was appointed as the Swedish diplomat to the Tsardom of R
2 CONC ussia under Ivan V of Russia.[1] On 8 October 1668 he sailed to Finland and then traveled overland to Narva. On 10 November 1668 he arrived at the Russian border at Orlina, where he was escorted to Novgorod. In his letter from Nov
2 CONC gorod to the Swedish government he writes: "Russians inevitably must be impelled to such unusual höflighetsbetygelser either under pressure from the enemy side or from fear of his friends." 
2 CONT
2 CONT He arrived in Moscow on 28 December 1668. His Swedish entourage consisted of 35 people. While in Russia, he made a copy of the Godunov map, one of two extant copies, the original by Petr Ivanovich Godunov having not survived. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Christina Ottiliana Börner and had a daughter, Charlotta Cronman, who married Henrik Gotthard von Buddenbrock (1648-1727). Charlotta and Henrick had as their son, Henrik Magnus Buddenbrock.
